    "q-90538": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90538",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=3,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{9}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}(6-x), & 3\\le x<6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9}(6-x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}(\\frac{1}{9}(6{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{36}|}_{0}^{3}+{\\frac{1}{9}(2{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-(3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
